"fq.eyebrow":"Frequently Asked Questions","fq.h2":"What patients ask most",
"fq.q1":"Can lymphedema be treated surgically?","fq.a1":"Lymphedema can be treated surgically from its early stages. Depending on the stage, lymphaticovenous anastomosis (LVA), vascularised lymph node transfer or debulking methods are planned alone or in combination. The appropriate method can only be determined after examination and imaging.",
"fq.q2":"What is the difference between lipedema and lymphedema?","fq.a2":"Lipedema is typically symmetric, affects both legs similarly and the swelling stops at the ankle, sparing the feet. Lymphedema is usually asymmetric and the swelling extends to the top of the foot and toes. In lipedema the tissue is often painful to touch, whereas in lymphedema the skin is tighter. A clinical examination is required for a definitive distinction.",
"fq.q3":"What does the health tourism authorisation certificate mean?","fq.a3":"The Turkish Ministry of Health's international health tourism authorisation is mandatory in order to treat patients coming from abroad. It is granted to providers and physicians meeting the Ministry's quality, staffing and inspection requirements, and ensures the patient is treated under legal safeguards.",
"fq.q4":"Do you accept international patients?","fq.a4":"Yes. Pre-assessment is carried out through an online consultation and the images you send; a personalised travel and treatment schedule is then planned. Patient communication in English is available.",
"fq.q5":"How is post-operative follow-up handled?","fq.a5":"Recovery is monitored through pre-scheduled check-ups. Online follow-up consultations are arranged for out-of-town and international patients, with liaison to a physiotherapy team in your own city where needed.",
"fq.q6":"What is microsurgery and why does it matter?","fq.a6":"Microsurgery is the transfer of tissue together with its blood vessels to another region, reconnecting vessels roughly 1 mm in diameter under a microscope. It is the most technical field of plastic surgery and makes both the reconstruction of lost tissue and the repair of the lymphatic system possible.",
